---
import SeoLayout from '../../layouts/SeoLayout.astro';
import { personaBySlug, isPublished, personaUrl, guideUrl } from '../../lib/seo-routes';
import { APP_URL } from '../../lib/site';

const persona = personaBySlug('webinar-organizers');
const title = 'Live translation for webinar organisers';
const description =
  'Run a webinar in several languages without prepaid hour blocks or a sales call. ' +
  'Published per-minute pricing, credits that never expire, no per-language tariff.';
---

<SeoLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={personaUrl(persona.slug)}
  noindex={!isPublished(persona)}
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <h1>{title}</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      Most webinar translation is sold in prepaid hour blocks, through a sales call, on an annual
      contract. That works if you run four conferences a year and can forecast the hours. It works
      badly if you announced Thursday's session on Tuesday.
    </p>

    <h2>What usually goes wrong</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The platform does less than assumed.</strong> Zoom translates to captions only — no translated
        audio. Teams Interpreter is not available in webinars or town halls at all. Google Meet supports
        one language pair per meeting, with a 90-minute cap.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The recording loses the translation.</strong> Microsoft's own documentation states that
        Teams recordings capture only the original meeting audio. For a session most people watch afterwards,
        that serves the smaller half of the audience.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The hours expire.</strong> Event vendors sell 10 to 50-hour minimums with 12-month expiry
        and no rollover. Irregular usage pays for hours it never uses.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we do differently</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Price published per minute.</strong> No sales call to find out what it costs.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Credits that never expire.</strong> An unused hour is still there next quarter.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No per-language tariff.</strong> A session in four languages is not four times a session
        in one.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When you should not choose us</h2>
    <p>
      If you are running a 5,000-person congress with simultaneous interpreters and a procurement
      process that expects SOC 2 and ISO 27001, an established event vendor is the right answer and
      we will say so. If your content is legally binding, hire a human interpreter. We serve the
      sessions that currently happen in one language because booking interpreters for them was never
      realistic.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to start</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href={guideUrl('how-to-run-a-multilingual-webinar')}>How to run a multilingual webinar</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={guideUrl('webinar-software-with-live-translation')}
          >Webinar software with live translation, compared honestly</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={guideUrl('do-i-need-an-interpreter-for-my-webinar')}
          >Do I need an interpreter for my webinar?</a
        >
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="cta"><a href={APP_URL}>Try it on a real session</a> — no contract, no minimum.</p>
  </article>
</SeoLayout>

<style>
  .wrap {
    max-width: 72ch;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 3rem 1.25rem;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }
  ul {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }
  li {
    margin-block: 0.5rem;
  }
  .cta {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
